<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="sec-gas4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Combined gas law</title>

	<p>
		Since pressure and volume are inversely proportional, volume and temperature
		are directly proportional, and pressure and temperature are directly
		proportional, we can P×V describe these three relationships with the equation
		= c.
		Since this relationship T should hold under multiple conditions (assuming that
		the amount of gas doesn’t change), we can derive the combined gas law: P1 × V1
		P2 × V2 = T1 T2
	</p>
	<exercises>
		<title>Exercise 1</title>

		<exercisegroup>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					Combined gas law (the following problems assume that the amount of gas
					doesn’t change)
				</p>
			</introduction>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						If a gas has a pressure of 17.0 atm, a volume of 1.05 L and a temperature
						of 302 K, what is...
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						If a gas has a pressure of 17.0 atm, a volume of 1.05 L and a temperature
						of 302 K, what is...
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						the new pressure if the volume is decreased to 0.55 L and the temperature
						is increased to 331 K?
						ii.
						the new volume if the pressure is increased to 34.0 atm and the
						temperature decreases to 151 K?
						iii.
						the new temperature if the pressure increases to 33.5 atm and the volume
						increases to 7.55 L
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						36 atm ii.
						0.263 L iii.
						4280 K
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>

			<exercise>
				<statement>
					<p>
						If a gas has a pressure of 1.52 MPa, a volume of 223 L and a temperature
						of 151 oC, what is the new temperature if the pressure decreases to 453
						kPa and the volume increases to 1.55 kL?
					</p>
				</statement>

				<answer>
					<p>
						879K
					</p>
				</answer>
			</exercise>
		</exercisegroup>
	</exercises>
</section>